+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a dependency or weighs a new service for larder. These tenets make the volunteers' capacity to
+operate it part of the choice. Written after [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md) reopened the same argument for the third
+time that year: Elasticsearch was proposed for slow, incomplete pantry search, and Postgres full-text search was
+reported to find the missing result in 40 ms instead of four seconds.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Prefer the services we already operate; add one only for a capability Postgres cannot provide and with two volunteers able to restore it.
+
+This rules out adding a service the existing database can cover, or one whose recovery depends on a single
+volunteer, even when someone can set it up quickly. A proposal for a service volunteers would operate must explain
+what Postgres cannot do and identify two volunteers able to restore the proposed service.
+
+The baseline is [larder's existing architecture](architecture.md). This tenet allows external capabilities such as
+the card gateway and hosted services that volunteers do not have to operate; it is not a blanket ban on services.
+
+Applied in [keeping sessions in the database](adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[running background jobs on Postgres](adr/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
